## 2026-05-24 — REVISION: canonical hash defect found + fixed

The 2026-05-19 linux smoke recorded a corpus_snapshot_hash of `a805cb47...`,
but that value was tainted by `rubric/__pycache__/*.pyc` files left over from
dev iteration — they (a) are not in git and (b) vary by Python patch version,
so the ADR-0011 §3.5 invariant could not hold across builders. Founder's
darwin smoke (`53265177...`) confirmed the defect on a different Python
(3.12.13 vs my 3.12.3).

Fix landed in commit 3215be6: `_canonical_corpus_hash` and `shutil.copytree`
now filter __pycache__/.pyc/.pyo/.DS_Store/.pytest_cache/.mypy_cache.

Corrected linux baseline (commit 3215be6, Python 3.12.3, x86_64-linux-gnu):
  binary_build_id:      build-20260524-x86_64-linux-gnu-3215be6d-partner-smoke
  binary_sha256:        79bf1d170c91cc862d998b9f3590afb0b9881b0fa305a7e5a6e172af23341ba8
  binary_size_bytes:    14774912
  corpus_snapshot_hash: c5f7b2516d1e67c9da0739373e8d4d94dc095f1ba219665341f332078ea85563
  source_date_epoch:    1779646817

This is the new canonical hash; darwin rebuild against the same commit must
produce the identical corpus_snapshot_hash.
